    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-verb\">Nobody</span> called, and I heard <span class=\"vp-prep\">nothing</span> all night.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>no + noun, none (of) instead of a noun.</strong> <em>We have no milk. / We have none. / None of my friends came.</em> Use <em>no</em> directly before a noun. Use <em>none</em> when the noun is left out, or before <em>of</em> + <em>them / us / the …</em>.",
        "tag": "no or none"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Nobody, nothing, nowhere and never are already negative.</strong> The verb after them is positive: <em>Nobody knows. / I saw nothing. / We went nowhere. / She never complains.</em> You can also say <em>no one</em> for <em>nobody</em>.",
        "tag": "One negative is enough"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>not + any- = a negative word.</strong> Both ways are correct, and they mean the same: <em>I saw nobody. = I didn't see anybody. / He said nothing. = He didn't say anything. / We have no time. = We haven't got any time.</em> Use one way or the other, never both.",
        "tag": "not any = no"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>Where does never go?</strong> Before the main verb, but after <em>be</em> and after the first auxiliary: <em>She never eats meat. / He is never late. / I have never been to Wales.</em> It does not go at the end of the sentence.",
        "tag": "never"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I didn't see nobody.</em> → ✓ <em>I saw nobody.</em> or <em>I didn't see anybody.</em> (two negatives) · ✗ <em>I haven't got no time.</em> → ✓ <em>I've got no time.</em> or <em>I haven't got any time.</em> · ✗ <em>We have none milk.</em> → ✓ <em>We have no milk.</em> / <em>We have none.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "In some dialects, and in many songs, people use two negatives for emphasis: <em>I ain't got nobody. / I can't get no satisfaction.</em> This is not standard English. Do not use it in an exam, at work or in writing. In standard English, two negatives can also cancel each other out: <em>It's not impossible</em> means <em>It is possible</em>. For the basic rules of <em>not</em> and <em>don't</em>, see <a href=\"/negation\">Negatives</a>. For <em>neither … nor</em>, see <a href=\"/zweiteilige-konnektoren\">Paired conjunctions</a>.",
